            <div xml:id="d724">

                <pb facs="1011" n="885" sameAs="frus:frus1925v01/pg885"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A 4 e/549</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Silas H. Strawn, of the American Delegation, to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-12-30"><hi rendition="#italic">December 30,
                        1925</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received January 28, 1926.]</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I may summarize what has
                    transpired since my letter of December 9th,<anchor n="85" xml:id="d724-f1"/> as
                    follows: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>On account of the war along the right of way, no train service between
                            Tientsin and Peking from December 8th until December 24th, since then
                            trains daily but requiring almost 24 hours to make the distance of 100
                            miles. This due to troop movement and war conditions.</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>No meetings of the Conference except two called by the Chinese to
                            demonstrate to the people that the Conference was still
                            functioning.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>With renewed assurances [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Silas H. Strawn</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
